  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Negresco is more than a hotel: it's a Nice landmark at 37 Promenade des Anglais, a member of The Leading Hotels of the World that, according to its website, has celebrated French art and heritage for over a century. The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office rates it 5 stars, with a spa, piano bar, restaurant, fitness room and equipped private beach, and states it is open every day.</p>

      <h2>With your dog</h2>
      <p>The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office says the hotel accepts pets, which must be specified when booking. The hotel's own website, in the pages consulted, does not detail this policy. A press article (Les Hardis, 26 August 2026) reports that dogs move around the rooms and common areas as long as they have been declared, with a blanket, a basket and two bowls on arrival, and meals prepared by the kitchen to the owner's instructions; this account does not come from the hotel. Expedia and Trip.com list €50 per pet per night and one pet per room, not confirmed by the hotel: neither this amount nor any weight limit is officially published.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: declare your dog when booking and ask in writing for the surcharge, the number of pets allowed per room and access to the restaurant and private beach.</div>

      <h2>On site</h2>
      <p>According to the tourist office the hotel has no car park of its own and refers to parking nearby; booking platforms mention a paid car park on reservation. Breakfast is charged up to €47 according to the tourist office. Check-in is from 3 pm and check-out before noon according to the platforms.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The tourist office places the beach within 300 m of the hotel. The Jardin Albert-Ier and Place Masséna are 800 m and 1.1 km away according to Planet of Hotels, and the Old Town is within walking distance to extend the outing with your dog. The rules for dogs on Nice's beaches and gardens should be checked on site.</p>
    </div>

